WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.440
Martial arts movies are their own brand of
fun.

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:06.479
Whether you're into well-choreographed action,
or just like watching chiseled warriors beat

00:00:06.479 --> 00:00:09.470
each other up, your viewing choices are better
than ever.

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:13.850
To help you stock up your kung fu queue, here
some lesser-known martial arts movie stars

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:17.040
from around the world who deserve way more
recognition.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.040
Ilram Choi

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:22.619
Described as one of Hollywood's "go-to stuntmen,"
Ilram Choi has been working behind the scenes

00:00:22.619 --> 00:00:24.710
in the industry for over a decade.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:29.239
Formally trained in taekwondo and a self-taught
master of gymnastics, Choi is responsible

00:00:29.239 --> 00:00:32.320
for countless movie stunts you've seen since
2002.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.530
Choi was one of Spider-Man's stunt doubles
in the Amazing Spider-Man movies.

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:39.940
In fact, he was so good at being Spider-Man,
he became the fight coordinator for Spider-Man:

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:40.969
Homecoming.

00:00:40.969 --> 00:00:45.480
Choi has done stunt work for a bunch of video
games, too, most notably serving as the basis

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:50.140
of Albert Wesker's fighting style in Resident
Evil 5 as well as doing some of the stunts

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:52.129
from Devil May Cry 3.

00:00:52.129 --> 00:00:53.750
Scott Adkins

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:58.070
This British actor is seemingly unable to
avoid being typecast as a Russian thug.

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:02.320
Trained mostly in kickboxing with a background
in taekwondo and gymnastics, Scott Adkins'

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.670
skills have recently been showcased during
his numerous fight scenes as Yuri Boyka, the

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:09.680
straight-to-DVD hero of the Undisputed movies.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.020
All in all, Adkins seems to feel most at home
making cheesy direct-to-video movies with

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:18.060
even cheesier names like Ninja: Shadow of
a Tear.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.840
Marko Zaror

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.170
Before he became an action star, Chilean actor
Marko Zaror initially made his foray into

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:26.149
the entertainment industry as a model.

00:01:26.149 --> 00:01:30.250
Standing around pouting in a pair of jeans
didn't cut it, so he decided to try his hands

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:32.630
at punching people in Spanish B-movies.

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:36.590
From this his transitioned to bit parts in
Hollywood and Bollywood movies before finally

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:41.070
returning to his native Chile to become the
country's first real action star.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:42.469
Vidyut Jammwal

00:01:42.469 --> 00:01:47.270
The big break for Vidyut Jammwal came courtesy
of Commando: A One Man Army, in which he performed

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:52.009
all of his own stunts and choreographed his
own fights specifically to show off his martial

00:01:52.009 --> 00:01:53.039
arts prowess.

00:01:53.039 --> 00:01:56.240
Most critics considered Jamwal to be the film's
only real saving grace.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.200
It was enough to get a sequel greenlit, which
looks even more ridiculous than the first

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.200
movie.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.369
In addition to being recognized as a phenomenal
martial artist, Jammwal was declared PETA's

00:02:05.369 --> 00:02:08.479
sexiest vegetarian in 2014.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:09.890
Lateef Crowder

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:14.280
For anyone with a passing familiarity with
the Tekken franchise, Lateef Crowder is basically

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:15.300
Eddy Gordo.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:19.110
In fact, Crowder's physical resemblance to
the character was so uncanny that he was the

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:22.360
only real choice to play him in the live-action
Tekken movie.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.379
"Bring it on boy.

00:02:24.379 --> 00:02:25.390
Come on!"

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:29.819
Along with appearing in terrible film adaptations
of popular video games, Crowder is a prolific

00:02:29.819 --> 00:02:33.010
extra in movies when they need a thug who
can can do cool spin kicks.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:37.510
As a result, footage exists of Crowder getting
beat up by everyone from Michael Jai White

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:39.840
to CGI wolves in the Twilight movies.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.400
Sadly for such a capable martial artist, Crowder
is criminally under-utilized in most of his

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:45.400
film roles.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:49.190
His greatest fight scene might just be his
earliest credited role in The Protector, which

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:51.550
saw him fighting against Tony Jaa.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:52.780
Wu Jing

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:57.250
Even though he's never gotten the same recognition
as guys like Donnie Yen or Jackie Chan, Wu

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:00.000
Jing is an astonishingly accomplished martial
artist.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.310
Known for his acrobatic fighting style, Jing
has mostly starred in Chinese action movies

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:07.380
with fun names like Metallic Attraction: Kungfu
Cyborg.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:16.480
Wu Jing's bread and butter seems to be taking
kicks and punches for a few minutes before

00:03:16.489 --> 00:03:18.920
ending his opponent with a 40-hit combo.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:23.740
As an OG of the martial arts world, Wu Jing
has appeared in everything from cheesy '90s

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:27.400
action movies to gritty war films, and he
always delivers.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.080
Johnny Nguyen

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:33.099
While Vietnamese martial artist Johnny Nguyen
has sporadically done stunt work in the U.S.

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:38.379
on projects like the original Spider-Man trilogy,
a live-action Mortal Kombat TV show, and X-Men:

00:03:38.379 --> 00:03:41.659
First Class, most of his appearances come
from Vietnamese action movies.

00:03:41.659 --> 00:03:45.379
If you've never heard of the guy and want
to ease into his kick-centric world, you could

00:03:45.379 --> 00:03:50.700
do worse than 2009's Clash, in which he kicks
three people in the face at the same time,

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.170
all while wearing mom jeans.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:53.760
Thanks for watching!

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.690
